<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Where Is Your Book? – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Where Is It?</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> Where Is Your Book?</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: text: Identify objects on the pages of picture books, using simple words.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can identify objects in picture books, using simple words (e.g., 'desk'). Can ask for help identifying objects by asking questions (e.g., 'What is this?'). Can confirm object names by repeating or responding to prompts (e.g., 'Is this a desk?').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by playing a "Where Is It?" song (e.g., a simple song about objects in a room with prepositions). Students listen and do TPR actions (e.g., pointing "up," "down," "on," "under"). The teacher then holds up a toy book and asks, "Where is the book?" while placing it in different locations (on the desk, under the chair). Students respond chorally "On the desk!" or "Under the chair!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models identifying objects and their locations using picture flashcards of a classroom scene. The teacher points to a 'book' and says, "This is a book. The book is on the desk." (pronouncing /b/ in 'book' clearly). Then, the teacher points to an object (e.g., a 'chair') and asks, "Where is the chair? The chair is next to the table." The teacher emphasizes the /w/ sound in 'where'. The teacher also models asking, "What is this?" and "Is this a desk?" to check understanding of mediation strategies.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a book on a desk in a picture) "Is the book under the desk? No!" (Shakes head) "It's on the desk."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a chair) "Is this a chair? Yes!" (Nods)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Where Is It?")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large, colorful picture book showing a classroom with various objects (desk, chair, book, bag, pencil, poster, shelf) in different locations. The book features simple sentences and large illustrations. This "text" will be mediated visually by the teacher for the students.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Using location words: Respond with basic words to location-based questions, using prepositions (e.g., 'The book is on the desk.'). Pragmatic: Following location commands: Move to designated areas in response to instructions (e.g., 'Go to the window.').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a page from the picture book. "Look! What do you see?" (Students point and say "book!" or "desk!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "Where is the book?" while pointing to it. Students say, "On the desk!" The teacher repeats and confirms, "Yes, the book is on the desk."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher says, "Show me the pencil." Students point to the pencil on the page. The teacher helps mediate by asking, "Is the pencil small?" "Yes!"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Prepositions of place (in, on, under, next to), WH-Questions for Location ('Where is...?'), Plural nouns in simple responses (e.g., 'three books').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> book, desk, chair, bag, pencil, marker, glue, ruler, shelf. Colors (yellow, red, blue).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Focus on /w/ in 'where' and /b/ in 'book'. Phonemic awareness: Recognizing letter sounds (K, L, M, N, O).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. "Match the Place" (Controlled practice):</b> The teacher provides each student with a small picture of an object (e.g., a book, a pencil, a bag) and a large poster with outlines of various classroom furniture (desk, chair, shelf). The teacher says, "Put the book on the desk!" Students physically place their object picture onto the corresponding outline. The teacher guides, "Is your book on the desk? Yes!" focusing on 'in', 'on', 'under'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. "Where's the Object?" (Semi-controlled):</b> Using a simplified drawing of a classroom (similar to the project map), the teacher points to an object (e.g., a red marker) and asks, "Where is the red marker?" Students respond chorally or individually, "It's in the box!" or "It's on the shelf!" The teacher then asks, "What is this?" pointing to a 'glue' and guiding students to say "Glue."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. "Tell Your Friend" (Pair interaction/Peer check):</b> Students work in pairs with two identical simplified classroom drawings, but one drawing has 2-3 objects slightly moved (e.g., 'pencil on desk' in one, 'pencil under desk' in other). Students take turns identifying the difference and mediating the information. Student A points to an object on their map and says, "My book is on the desk." Student B checks their map and says, "My book is on the desk too!" or "My book is under the desk." (Teacher circulates and provides support and prompts like "What is this?"). Students practice asking politely, "Show me the blue crayon, please."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Where Is It?")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative classroom map creation and presentation. This is the culmination of the "Where's My Bag?" 21st Century Skills Project.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will work in small groups (2-3 students) to create a simple classroom map. The teacher provides large sheets of paper with pre-drawn outlines of major classroom furniture (tables, chairs, shelves, teacher's desk). Students receive small cut-out pictures of classroom objects (books, pencils, bags, folders, markers, rulers, glue – from the vocabulary list) and glue sticks. They will collaboratively place and glue the objects onto the map, reflecting their actual locations or designated spots, and verbally mediate the location information to their group members and then to the class.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group receives a base map and a bag of object cut-outs.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reminds students of the project: "Let's make our classroom map: Where's My Bag?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work together to place the object cut-outs onto their map outlines. As they place each object, they verbally mediate its location using prepositions: "The book is on the desk." "My bag is under the chair." (Teacher monitors and prompts "Where is the pencil?" or "Is the glue on the shelf?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Once objects are glued, each group briefly presents their map to the class. One student points to an object (e.g., 'bag') and another student from the group says its location "It's under the table." A third student can ask a peer in another group, "What is this?" about an object on their map, or "Show me the yellow ruler, please."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The focus is on students mediating information about locations through visual representation (the map) and simple verbal descriptions.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment, student self-reflection)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist. The teacher uses a simple checklist to note students' ability to identify objects and use prepositions during their group work and presentation of the classroom map.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their completed classroom map, pointing to objects and stating their locations using simple sentences and prepositions (e.g., "The red book is on the desk."). They respond to teacher or peer questions like "Where is the bag?" or "Is this a marker?".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Identifies objects:</b> Can point to and name at least 3-4 classroom objects correctly (e.g., book, desk, chair, bag).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Uses prepositions:</b> Can use at least 2-3 prepositions (on, in, under) to describe location on the map.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Answers location questions:</b> Responds to "Where is the...?" with basic phrases.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Asks for help/confirms:</b> Attempts to ask "What is this?" or "Is this a...?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Works with group members to complete the map.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Clear enough pronunciation of /w/ in 'where' and /b/ in 'book'.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Show a smiley face/sad face drawing) "Did you help make the map? (Yes/No gesture)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you say 'on the desk'?" (Students repeat or give a thumbs up/down).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite part of making our classroom map today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you tell me where your pencil is right now?" (Encourage them to use "on" or "in").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this mediation lesson? The collaborative map-making really engaged students in applying prepositions and object identification. Their ability to explain where objects were on the map demonstrated their mediation skills at an A1.2 level.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for next time? Some students needed more prompting for plural nouns ("three books"). Next time, I could provide sentence frames more explicitly ("The ____ is ____ the ____.") or use a chant to reinforce plural nouns with locations.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "Where Is Your Book?" theme. The next theme will build upon spatial awareness and vocabulary in a new context, potentially using the 'classroom map' as a reference point for new activities, applying the learned location concepts to different environments or situations.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>